            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>Air Sealing



&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

The air leakage in the house was quite low, but there are a few areas which could use improvement. The amount of leakage was tested to be about 1.5 air changes per hour (ACH) at 50 Pa, which is the standard unit for measuring air leakage.



The majority of air leakage is coming from the crawlspace. There are pipes and wires that travel through the walls; these are location where air leakage can occur. Spray foam is useful for big gaps and caulking can be used for smaller gaps. Also air can leak in between the foundation and the crawlspace wall; this could be another area that could use a bead of caulking.



The front double door also likely is quite leaky. The blower door equipment was set up in this door during the test but the seal around the door could be improved. If the double door isnt needed then it could be replaced with a single exterior door.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 130&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</airSeal>
                <ceilings>Ceilings (Gable)



The attic of your house was assumed to have 12 inches (R40) fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 6 more inches (R20) fiberglass batt insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 6 inches (R20) fiberglass batt insulation to the attic, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 50&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;1.9 GJ&lt;/b. There are no rebates available if your attic insulation is already above R40.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</ceilings>
                <HotWaterSystem>DWHR 



Drain water heat recovery systems can capture heat from showers and put that heat back into your hot water tank. The waste water is not reused, but the heat from it is collected by the cold water coming in. These systems are typically a copper pipe with a smaller copper coil wrapped around it. It is placed into a vertical section of the drain stack and is a passive way to save energy.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a drain water heat recovery pipe with an efficiency of at least 30%, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 105&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 700 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;





---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>COMBUSTION AIR



There is an open combustion air supply pipe to providing air to the boiler and hot water tank.

The combustion air pipe should have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the boiler or hot water tank is operating.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing an electronic damper on your combustion supply, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 660&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;25.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to learn the systems usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house you can qualify for &lt;b&gt;$ 175 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HeatingSystem>
                <WaterConservation>WATER CONSERVATION



I recommend you replace all toilets. When replacing your toilets, purchase 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilets. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing your toilets with new 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilet(s), you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save ~$50 &lt;/b&gt; per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>ADDITIONAL COMMENTS



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Enterprise&lt;/b&gt;

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $18.60 min, $0.30 per kWh

Oil  - $0.93 per litre 

Propane - $0.50 per litre

Wood - $250 per cord 

Pellets - $6.25 per bag



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Your Energy Advisor&lt;/b&gt;



